The piezo-phototronic effect can effectively modulate the energy band structure at the interface of p-n or metalsemiconductor junction, manipulating the separation, transport, and recombination of photoinduced charges. While zinc oxide $\left(\mathrm{ZnO}\right)$ is a good electron transporting layer (ETL), its Lewis’s basic nature and presence of surface defects lead to deprotonation of perovskite, resulting in severe degradation of perovskite solar cells (PSC). Herein, the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface is converted to $z\mathrm{n}s$ , which acts as a bifunctional interfacial layer, passivating the ZnO/perovskite interface by reducing the hydroxyl $(-\mathrm{OH})$ group on the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface for improved stability and forming strong coordination with $\mathbf{Pb^{2+}}$ of perovskite $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{Zn-S-Pb}$ pathway) and thus adjusting the energy level for efficient electron transport. Consequently, the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of the flexible PSC is remarkably improved from $12.94\%$ to $14.68\%$ under static external strain of $1.5\%$ , ascribed to the strain-induced piezo-polarization charges, which modulate the energy band structure of $z\mathrm{ns}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ and ZnS/perovskite in­ terfaces. As a result, spatial separation of photoinduced carriers is facilitated, reducing recombination proba­ bility. The energy band diagram is proposed to elucidate the mechanism. This strategy enables effective utilization of the piezo-phototronic effect while enhancing device stability.
## 1. Introduction
The piezo-phototronic effect has attracted significant attention as an effective new strategy to enhance the performance of optoelectronics, leading to the emergence of novel fundamental phenomena and unparalleled applications [1–4]. In piezo-phototronics, optical and electrical properties are coupled with piezoelectric properties, gener­ ating strain-induced polarization charges upon mechanical deformation, which effectively changes the energy band structure at interfaces, such as p-n junction and metal-semiconductor Schottky contact [5]. This way, piezo-phototronics can manipulate the photogenerated charge carrier’s separation, transport, and recombination processes [6–9].
The performance enhancement of optoelectronic devices, such as solar cells [9–12], light-emitting diodes [13–15], and photodetectors [1, 4,16–18], through piezo-phototronic effect demonstrates the ability to control power conversion efficiency (PCE), light-emitting intensity, and light responsivity [5]. A piezopotential is generated from a change in the dipole moment of a noncentral symmetric wurtzite semiconductor in response to mechanical deformation, and its magnitude depends on chemical constituents, steric orientation, and crystal deformation under stress. Semiconductors, such as GaN [19,20], $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ [21,22], CdS [23, 24], and ZnO [25–28], have a wurtzite structure with noncentral sym­ metric crystal, which possesses piezoelectric properties, thus generating internal piezopotential under mechanical deformation.
Among these semiconductors, $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , a promising optoelectronic ma­ terial with extraordinary electron transport properties, has extensively been studied as an electron transporting layer (ETL) in perovskite solar cells (PSC) and for performance enhancement of PSC using the piezophototronic effect, summarized in Table S1. For example, Hu et al. fabricated flexible PSC devices utilizing piezoelectric $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ microwire to form a heterojunction with perovskite. The corresponding open-circuit voltage $\mathrm{(V_{oc})}$ , short-circuit current $(\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{sc}})$ and PCE improved by $25.42\%$ , $629.47\%$ and $1280\%$ , respectively [6]. Similarly, Sun et al. fabricated $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods (NRs) based flexible PSC with improved PCE from $9.3\%$ to $12.8\%$ under a static mechanical strain of $1.88\%$ via the piezo-phototronic effect [29]. Despite the performance enhancement, device stability remains a concern due to the Lewis’s basic nature of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and the presence of surface defects, such as hydroxyl and oxygen va­ cancies, leading to deprotonation of perovskite [30]. It is reported that methylammonium $\mathbf{(MA+)}$ cations in perovskite film are easily deprotonated by $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , releasing methylamine at temperature $>90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ resulting in severe degradation of PSC [31]. Consequently, ZnO-based flexible PSC could not reach a high PCE, varying between $2.62\%$ and $17.1\%$ (Table S2) compared to flexible PSC based on other ETL such as $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ which have reached a record efficiency of $20.7\%$ [32].
The instability issue can be overcome by either doping $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ with metal ions $(\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ , ${\mathsf{C}}s^{+}.$ ) [33,34] or passivation with organ­ ic/inorganic materials [35–37] to obstruct direct contact between ZnO and perovskite. For passivation, various strategies have been proposed to improve the stability of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ based PSC. Azmi et al. introduced a self-assembled monolayer (SAM) on $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ to passivate surface hydroxyl vacancies [35]. Cheng et al. report buffer layer passivation using poly­ ethyleneimine (PEI) and [6,6]-phenyl-C61-butyric acid methyl ester $(\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM})$ to enhance the stability and work function of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ based PSC [38]. However, these strategies cannot ensure complete surface passiv­ ation, resulting in limited stability enhancement. Also, the disparity of the passivated layer between $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and perovskite does not allow effec­ tive utilization of piezo-potential charges under external mechanical strain. It was reported that a complete surface coverage with improved stability of $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ can be achieved using a core-shell structure [36,37]. Chen et al. reported a UV stable rigid mesoporous PSC with $z_{\mathrm{nO-ZnS}}$ annealed at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ as ETL with improved stability $(87\%$ for $500~\mathrm{{h}}{\mathrm{{i}}}$ and PCE of $20.7\%$ [36]. Li et al. reported core-shell $\mathsf{Z n O@S n O_{2}}$ nano­ particles ETL-based PSC, exhibiting PCE of $14.35\%$ [37]. However, the fabrication requires a high annealing temperature $(450^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , which is not suitable for flexible PSC. Thus, a combined strategy to overcome the instability issue with effective utilization of the piezo-phototronic effect to enhance the performance of flexible PSC has not been reported.
In this study, ZnO nanorods are grown on flexible ITO/PET substrate by a hydrothermal method and post-treated with sulfide to convert ZnO nanorods surface to $z\mathrm{n}s$ layer as cascade ETL for PSC. The ZnS shell acts as a passivation interlayer between $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods and perovskite film forming pathways for efficient electron transport due to strong coordi­ nation of sulfide with the $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ and thereby suppresses the ‒OH group on the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface, resulting in highly stable PSC. Furthermore, with a static tensile strain of $1.5\%$ , the PCE of the device improves from $12.94\%$ to $14.68\%$ and Jsc from 21.82 to $24.40\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The improvement is attributed to the strain-induced piezo-polarization charges at $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ core and $z\mathrm{n}s$ shell layer, which modulates the energy band structure at both $z\mathrm{ns}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ and ZnS/perovskite interfaces. This study provides a general approach to improve the stability and PCE of flexible PSC in wearable and portable electronic devices.
## 2. Results and discussion
## 2.1. Surface conversion of ZnO nanorods to ZnS for flexible PSC
$z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods were grown on flexible ITO/PET substrate by a lowtemperature hydrothermal method followed by treatment with thio­ acetamide solution to obtain a core-shell structure (see detail in Section 4) [27,39]. A schematic illustration of the fabrication process is shown in Fig. 1a. The crystal phase and composition of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods and core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs are studied through X-ray diffraction (XRD), as shown in Fig. 1b. The spectrum of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods shows a sharp diffraction peak (002) at $34.4^{\circ}$ , indicating a hexagonal wurtzite struc­ ture with preferential orientation along c-axis. Sulfidation of the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods induces a new peak at $28.5^{\circ}$ (along 001), endorsing a $z\mathrm{n}s$ shell layer formation on $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ core [40]. As the sulfidation time increases, the ratio of the characteristic peaks of $z\mathrm{n}s$ increases, indicating continuous etching of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ core and conversion to $z\mathrm{n}s$ . The bandgap energy $(E g)$ of ZnO nanorods and core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs is obtained from ultraviolet-visible (UV–vis) absorption spectra as shown in Fig. 1c. The absorption peak in $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods spectra is located at $361~\mathrm{{nm}}$ contrib­ uting to $\scriptstyle{E g}$ of $3.26\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. S1), which is close to the reported value of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods [41]. The absorption spectra of core-shell $\mathbf{ZnO}@\mathbf{ZnS}$ NRs show two characteristic absorption peaks at 359.5 and $312.5~\mathrm{nm}$ cor­ responding to $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods and $z\mathrm{n}s$ layer, respectively, which confirms the formation of core-shell structure. Moreover, the absorption intensity of ZnS peak at $312.5\mathrm{nm}$ is higher than ZnO, indicating the formation of ZnS shell layer on the surface of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ core. The calculated Eg for core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO@ZnS~NRs}\_{0.5}$ is 3.39 eV (Fig. S1), resulting from the bandgap interaction between ZnS and ZnO. Core-shell $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{ZnO@2nS}}$ NRs_1.0 and core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO@2nS}$ NRs_1.5 show Eg of 3.44 and $3.48\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. As sulfidation time increases, the ZnS peak becomes more dominant and the $\scriptstyle{E g}$ becomes close to the reported value of ZnS [42].
To further ensure the formation of the hexagonal wurtzite structure of ZnO with and without ZnS, field emission scanning electron micro­ scopy (FE-SEM) was conducted, as shown in Fig. 2. Fig. 2a and b shows even and uniform morphology of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods with hexagonal struc­ ture and growth orientation along the c-axis with a diameter of $\sim40\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $\sim1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ length [43]. This is further confirmed by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and the corresponding selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern, which illustrate that the as-fabricated $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods have a single crystals hexagonal structure and that the vertical growth plane is along the c-axis (002) as shown in Fig. 2c. During sul­ fidation, the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface is etched along with ZnS growth forming a rough shell layer on $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ core. With $0.5\mathrm{h}$ of sulfidation, the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface becomes fully covered with a thin layer of $z\mathrm{n}s$ , retaining $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods morphology (Fig. 2d and e). Increasing the sulfidation time, the $z\mathrm{n}s$ shell layer thickens, and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ core diminishes as more O is replaced by S (Fig. S2). The TEM image confirms that the $z\mathrm{n}s$ nanoparticles are well distributed on the surface throughout the length of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods as shown in Fig. 2f. The corresponding ring-like SAED pattern is indexed to polycrystalline $z\mathrm{n}s$ with reflections assigned to (111), (200), (220), and (311) planes of cubic zinc blende-type ZnS (inset of Fig. 2f) [44]. The core-shell structure with $0.5\mathrm{h}$ sulfidation retained the wurtzite morphology of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ core and is thus optimal for further analysis.
## 2.2. Effect of ZnS on ZnO surface modification and energy level alignment
There are two kinds of surface hydroxyl group (‒OH) exist on metal oxides with amphoteric characteristic: terminal group, which binds to metal site with basic characteristic, and bridge group, which binds to metal site with acidic characteristic [45]. The basic terminal group $(-$ OH) on $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface leads to the deprotonation of organic amine in perovskite causing severe degradation of PSC [46]. Surface conversion of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ to $z\mathrm{ns}$ only passivates the ZnO/perovskite interface but also decreases or eliminates ‒OH groups from ZnO surface.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) of pristine $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ nanorods and core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs_0.5 was performed as shown in Fig. 3. Fig. 3a depicts the full range survey spectrum of pristine $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods with $Z\mathbf{n}$ and O characteristic peaks and core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs_0.5 with Zn, O and S characteristic peaks. The $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ region in pristine ZnO and ZnS modified sample shows two peaks at \~ 1044.6 $(2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ and \~ 1021.6 eV $\mathrm{(2p_{3/2})}$ ) corresponding to $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ state with spin-orbit splitting energy of $23\mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 3b). The XPS spectra of O 1s for pristine $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods show two distinct peaks for different oxygen species. A peak at $531.6\mathrm{eV}$ indicates the presence of ‒OH group on the ZnO surface, and another peak at $530.7\mathrm{eV}$ indicates $\mathsf{O}^{2-}$ state originating from saturated oxygen in $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (Fig. 3c). On the other hand, the core-shell $\mathbf{ZnO}@\mathbf{ZnS}$ NRs_0.5 show two O 1s peaks at 529.7 and $528.2\:\mathrm{eV}$ , assigned to chemisorbed oxygen species of surface $-\mathrm{OH}$ and crystal lattice $\mathsf{O}^{2-}$ ions in $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , respectively (Fig. 3d) [47]. By comparing the XPS spectra of O 1s, the ratio of ‒OH to $\mathsf{O}^{2-}$ is reduced from 1.23 to 0.38 [48]. As a result, the $z\mathrm{n}s$ interlayer reduces the $-\mathrm{OH}$ functionality of the surface of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods, which is beneficial for stability improvement. Further, while no $\mathtt{s2p}$ peak is observed in $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods spectra (Fig. S3), two $\mathtt{s2p}$ peaks are observed in core-shell ZnO@ZnS NRs_0.5 at $\sim161.1$ eV and $\sim$ 160.07 eV (Fig. 3e), assigned to $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ and $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ respectively. This confirms the formation of $Z\mathrm{n}{-}S$ bond, where $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface is converted to ZnS, resulting in a core-shell structure as shown in Fig. 2d–f [49,50].
Ultraviolet photoemission spectroscopy (UPS) analysis was carried out to determine the electronic structure of the valence band of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnS}~\mathrm{NRs}_{-}0.5$ as shown in (Fig. 3f–i). The valence band maximum (VBM, $E_{o n s e t})$ of core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO@ZnS~NRs}\_{0.5}$ lies below the fermi level at $3.5\mathrm{eV}$ , obtained by simulating a straight line into the leading edge (Fig. 3f). Whereas the secondary electronic cutoff $(E_{c u t o f f})$ energy lies on the right side of the spectrum at $17.5\mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 2g). The energy level of VBM $(E_{V B M})$ , calculated using the equation $E_{V B M}=21.22$ $-\ (E_{c u t o f f}-E_{o n s e t})$ [51,52] of core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnS}~\mathrm{NRs}_{-}0.5$ is $-7.22\mathrm{eV}$ . The calculated work function of core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnSNR}s\_{0.5}$ is $3.72\mathrm{eV}$ , obtained by subtracting the $E_{c u t o f f}$ from the excitation energy $(21.22\mathrm{eV})$ .
The conduction band maximum (CBM) was obtained using the equation, $E_{C B M}=E_{V B M}+E g$ [53], where $E_{C B M}$ is the energy level of CBM and $\scriptstyle{E g}$ is the bandgap derived from UV–vis spectroscopy (Fig. S1). Thus, the calculated $E_{C B M}$ of core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO@2nS}$ NRs_0.5 is $-3.83\mathrm{eV}$ . Similarly, $E_{o n s e t}$ and $E_{c u t o f f}$ of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods are $3.4\mathrm{eV}$ and $17.3\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively (Fig. 3h and i). Eg, $E_{V B M,}$ and $E_{C B M}$ of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods are 3.26, $-7.32$ , and $-4.07\ \mathrm{eV}.$ , respectively. It is found that the core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnS}\mathrm{NRs}_{-}0.5$ have higher upshift of the CBM edge, leading to the smallest energy difference between the ETL and PSC. The upshift of HOMO level con­ tributes to efficient extraction of photogenerated electrons from perov­ skite, which is beneficial to boost device performance.
## 2.3. Photovoltaic performance of strain-free flexible PSC
The schematic device structure and cross-sectional SEM image of PSC are shown in Fig. 4a. ZnO nanorods and core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs were grown on ITO/PET substrate as ETL, then the perovskite film was spin coated on ETL layer followed by Spiro-OMeTAD and growth of silver layer as a hole transporting layer (HTL) and back metal contact, respectively. The energy level diagram, constructed based on EVBM and $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{CBM}}$ values (Fig. 4b), shows that $\mathbf{E_{CBM}}$ of $z_{\mathrm{n}S}$ is between that of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods and perovskite [54,55], where the photoexcited electrons in the conduction band of perovskite cascade into $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods through $z\mathrm{n}s$ . Based on the energy band alignment theory, the energy barrier at the $z\mathrm{ns}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ interface would effectively lower the charge recombina­ tion of photoexcited carriers and thus increase the quasi-Fermi level for electrons, leading to higher open-circuit voltage (Voc) [36].
The surface morphology of perovskite film on ETL was studied by AFM (Fig. 4c) and SEM (Fig. S4). AFM image shows a smooth surface with large perovskite grain is obtained on core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnS}\mathrm{NRs}_{-}0.5$ with a roughness (Ra) of $9.57\mathrm{nm}$ . Further, the perovskite grains are compact and without pinholes, forming a defect free film (Fig. S4). Whereas the perovskite grains on $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods are smaller with an irregular morphology (Fig. S5). Thus, the core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnS}~\mathrm{NRs}_{-}0.5$ layer is considered as a superior ETL compared to ZnO nanorods [56]. The perovskite film on ETL was further characterized by UV–vis spec­ troscopy (Fig. S6). The optical absorption spectra of perovskite on ZnO and core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnS}\ \mathrm{NRs}_{-}0.5$ film are very similar, indicating both films have similar composition and thickness [57].
To understand the interlayer effect of $z_{\mathrm{n}S}$ on the photovoltaic per­ formance of PSC device, the current density-open circuit voltage (J–V) curve under $\mathsf{A M1.5G}$ illumination of the core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO@ZnS~NRs}\_{0.5}$ and $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ nanorods device was studied (Fig. 4d). The PCE in reverse scan (RS) of the control device is $11.97\%$ with Voc of $0.89\mathrm{V}$ , Jsc of
$21.40\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and fill factor (FF) of $62\%$ . A significant improvement with core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnSNR}s\_{0.5}$ is observed in PCE with RS of $12.94\%$ , Jsc of $21.82\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , Voc of $0.94\mathrm{V}$ and FF of $63\%$ under the same measurement conditions. The detailed parameters for forward scan (FS) and RS are shown in Table S3. The core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs_0.5 device showed negligible hysteresis with PCE of FS $12.70\%$ , Jsc of $21.48~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , Voc of $0.95\mathrm{V}_{:}$ , and FF of $63\%$ , whereas the PCE of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods device in FS is $10.74\%$ , Jsc of $21.70\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , Voc of $0.87\mathrm{V}$ and FF of $56\%$ . While Voc is significantly improved with core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs_0.5, Jsc of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods and core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ $\mathrm{NRs}\ 0.5$ is comparable, indicating that the $z\mathrm{n}s$ shell does not hinder the transfer of photoexcited electrons from perovskite to the electrode via $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ core [57]. The improved FF of core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnS}\mathrm{NRs}_{-}0.5$ implies more efficient electron extraction and transfer with suppressed recom­ bination. The external quantum efficiency (EQE) is greater than $85\%$ for core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnS~NRs}_{-}0.5$ devices in the visible region as shown in Fig. 4e. The photocurrent densities calculated from the EQE spectrum of core-shell $Z\mathrm{nO}@\mathrm{ZnS~NRs}_{-}0.5/$ perovskite and ZnO nanorods/perovskite are $21.07\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and $20.37\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , respectively. This is in good agreement with the current densities calculated from the J–V curves, with only $3.43\%$ mismatch in core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs_0.5 devices, compared to $4.81\%$ for $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods devices. Since the perovskite surface morphology is analogous for $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and core-shell $\mathbf{ZnO@ZnS}$ NRs_0.5, the improvement in device performance is mainly attributed to the modification of the interfacial optoelectronic properties. Further­ more, the thickness of the ZnS shell is more critical to device perfor­ mance. The thickness of the $z_{\mathrm{n}S}$ layer is controlled by varying the sulfidation time. In particular, the optimal $z\mathrm{n}s$ layer at $0.5\mathrm{h}$ sulfidation resulted in maximum Voc, indicating full coverage of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ core with a thin ZnS shell layer (Fig. 2e) [57]. With increasing the thickness of $z\mathrm{n}s$ shell layer with 1.0 and $1.5\mathrm{h}$ , the Voc increases but Jsc drops at the same time (Fig. S7). Moreover, the statistical photovoltaic parameters from 20 strain-free flexible PSC devices for each sample demonstrating the reproducibility of the performance enhancement associated with the use of ZnS interlayer as shown in Fig. S8. The control device based on $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods as ETL show an average PCE of $11.68\%$ , whereas core-shell $\mathbf{ZnO@ZnS}$ NRs with 0.5, 1.0 and $1.5\mathrm{h}$ of sulfidation time show an average PCE of $12.93\%$ , $11.68\%$ and $11.12\%$ , respectively. The enhancement of core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO@ZnS~NRs}\_{0.5}$ confirm the advantage of thin $z\mathrm{n}s$ interlayer over control ZnO nanorods. This is also in accordance with the trend of high perovskite film quality and efficient extraction as deduced from UPS analysis.
Steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved PL (TRPL) spectroscopies were used to investigate the charge-transfer kinetics at the ETL/perovskite interface. Fig. 4f shows the PL spectra of perovskite on $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ nanorods and core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs_0.5. The PL intensity of perovskite on core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnSNR}s_{-}0.5$ is less than one-third on ZnO nanorods, indicating a more substantial quenching effect of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nano­ rods with ZnS interlayer [36,57]. The quenching phenomenon is endorsed by TRPL results and the corresponding carrier lifetimes were calculated using the fitting biexponential equation: $Y=Y_{0}+A_{1}$ exp $(-t/\tau_{1})\ +\ \mathrm{A}_{2}\ \exp(-t/\tau_{2})$ (Fig. $_{4g)}$ . While $(\tau_{1})$ is the faster decay component indicating a non-radiative recombination, $(\tau_{2})$ is the slower decay component indicating radiative recombination. The fitted pa­ rameters are summarized in Table S4. The TRPL results show that the electron transfer across the perovskite/core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs_0.5 interface is faster $(\uptau_{1}=0.116\upmu s)$ than that of perovskite/ZnO nanorods interface $(\uptau_{1}=0.34\upmu s)$ . This is due to matching of energy level of the core-shell $\mathbf{ZnO@ZnS}$ NRs_0.5 and perovskite, enhancing the electron extraction and reducing the extraction time.
## 2.4. Piezo-phototronic effect on the performance of flexible PSC
The output enhancement of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods and core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO@2nS}$
To further confirm that the enhancement is not due to change in the effective illumination area during bending, EQE was measured in flexed mode at a tensile strain of $1.5\%$ for core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\textcircled{a}}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs_0.5 (Fig. S11) and compared with that in strain free mode (Fig. 4e), showing no shift in the absorption edge. This suggests that promotion of photo­ generated carriers and improvement in the photovoltaic performance of PSC devices under tensile strain is attributed to modulated carrier transport behavior due to piezoelectric potential and the piezopolarization charges generated at the $\scriptstyle{{\mathrm{ZnS/ZnO}}}$ and ZnS/perovskite in­ terfaces. As a result, the junction barrier height at both interfaces is modulated, facilitating the spatial separation of the photoinduced car­ riers as well as remarkably suppressing the recombination rate, leading to improved performance. To confirm the suppression in charge recombination electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was per­ formed in strain mode (Fig. S12). The EIS results show the contact resistance (Rco) remains constant with increasing tensile strain from $0\%$ to $2\%$ , whereas the recombination resistance (Rrec) increased with increasing strain from $0\%$ to $1.5\%$ indicating effective suppression of charge recombination. On the other hand, at $2\%$ tensile strain, Rrec started to decrease and consequently Jsc and PCE declined. This indicates that increase in tensile strain above $1.5\%$ , Rrec increases, which is consistent with a previous report showing maximum enhancement at $1.88\%$ tensile strain [29]. Furthermore, the Voc and FF values are shown in Fig. S13. With increase in tensile strain, Voc is marginally improved since Voc is decided by quasi fermi level at the junction and is dependent on the illumination intensity, charge carrier concentration, and bandgap [58,59]. The equation for Voc change with illumination intensity is given by Eqs. (1) and (2):
$$
V_{o c}\approx\bigg(\frac{n k T}{q}\bigg)\times l n\bigg(\frac{J_{s c}}{J_{o}}\bigg)
$$
$$
d(V_{o c})\approx(n k T J_{o})\times\left(\frac{d(J_{s c})}{J_{s c}}\right)
$$
where n, $\mathtt{K T/q}$ , and $\mathbf{J}_{0}$ are the ideality factor (so-called diode-ideality factor), thermal voltage, and dark reverse saturation current, respec­ tively. Eqs. (1) and (2) show that change in Jsc has direct influence on Voc. At ambient temperature, $\mathrm{{nKT/q}}$ and $\mathrm{Jsc/J_{o}}$ are $\sim300\mathrm{mV}$ and $\sim$ 500, respectively. Thus, a change of $1\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in Jsc will lead to a change of 0.0001 V. similarly, the effect of Voc on carrier concentration is given in Eq. (3):
$$
V_{o c}\approx\frac{k T}{q}\times l n\left(\frac{(N_{A}+\Delta n)\Delta n}{{n_{i}}^{2}}\right)
$$
$$
F F=\quad\frac{V_{o c}-l n(V_{o c}+0.72)}{V_{o c}+1}
$$
The PCE (η) of PSC is defined in Eqs. (5) and (6) [61]:
$$
\begin{array}{l l}{P C E,}&{\displaystyle\eta=\frac{P_{o u t}}{P_{i n}}}\\ {P C E,}&{\displaystyle\eta_{c a l}=\frac{V_{o c}\times J_{s c}\times F F}{P_{i n}\times A_{c a l}}}\end{array}
$$
where $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{cal}}$ and $\mathbf{P_{\mathrm{in}}}$ are the calibrated effective illumination area (the change in area exposed to illumination when strain is applied) and the light intensity illuminated on the PSC devices (The detail procedure for $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{cal}}$ is illustrated in Fig. S14). An improvement in the PCE of flexible PSC is observed at a tensile strain of $1.5\%$ due to significant improvement of
Jsc. The results show that the piezo-phototronic effect significantly improves the PCE of flexible PSC due to Jsc enhancement.
For further insight into the piezo-phototronic effect on the flexible PSC, an external compression strain is applied on the device by convex bending as shown in Fig. 6. The J–V curves of the device at different compression strain for ZnO and core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{ZnS}\mathrm{NRs}_{-}0.5$ are shown in Fig. 6a and c. A decrease in photovoltaic parameters occurs with increasing compression strain, as a result, the PCE drops [29]. The maximum compression strain applied to devices was $-2\%_{;}$ upon which the PCE of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods based PSC devices decreased from $11.97\%$ to $9.88\%$ and Jsc decreased from 21.40 to $18.30\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Fig. 6b). Similarly, the PCE and Jsc of core-shell $\mathbf{ZnO@ZnS}$ NRs_0.5 based PSC decreased from $12.94\%$ to $10.69\%$ and 21.82 to $18.50\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively (Fig. 6d). However, in both devices a negligible decrease is observed in Voc and FF of PSC with increasing compression strain (Fig. S15). Whereas the core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs_1.0 and core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO@ZnS~NRs}_{-}1.5$ show a decline in PCE from $11.7\%$ to $10.36\%$ and $11.17\%$ to $9.84\%$ , respectively, with increasing tensile strain from $0\%$ to $-2\%$ as shown in Fig. S16. Furthermore, to confirm the decline of photovoltaic performance with increasing compression strain, 8 inde­ pendent PSC devices for each strain were tested and the statistical results of Jsc and PCE are shown in Fig. S17. The PSC based on different ETL demonstrate an overall decline in Jsc, resulting in decrease in PCE from
$0\%$ to $-2\%$ strain. The decline in performance is due to increase in charge recombination as confirmed by EIS (Fig. S12). Rco and Rrec were obtained from the Nyquist plot with varying strain from $0\%$ to $-2\%$ While Rco remained constant with increasing compression strain, Rrec decreased with increasing strain, indicating favorable charge recombi­ nation and as a result the PCE substantially decreased.”
## 2.5. Energy band structure modulation via piezoelectric potential
A schematic illustration showing the mechanism of the piezophototronic effect in enhancing the performance of flexible PSC with $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods and core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs as ETL, the corresponding energy band diagram, and its realignment induced by piezo-potential distribution under external strains are shown in Figs. S22 and Fig. 7 [29,59,62]. A representative model device with strain-free, tensile, and compression strains is shown in Fig. 7a, d and g. The flexible PSC device with $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ nanorods or core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs as ETL functions in three steps; (a) when the device is illuminated with $\mathrm{AM}1.5\mathrm{G}$ , photoinduced carriers are generated in perovskite; (b) separation of photogenerated carrier and transfer toward respective electrodes (electrons are trans­ ferred to cathode through ETL and holes are transferred to anode through HTL) and (c) recombination of photogenerated carriers at ETL/perovskite interface, HTL/perovskite interface, or grain boundaries as shown in Fig. S22 [63]. Among which, the most critical step is the separation of photoinduced carriers, where faster separation reduces recombination probability and enhances device performance. Charge recombination is more dominant at ETL and HTL/perovskite interfaces than grain boundaries, leading to drop in Voc [64–66]. The energy level diagram shows that $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{CBM}}$ of ZnS is between that of ZnO nanorods and perovskite [54,55], where the photoexcited electrons in the conduction band of perovskite cascade into $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ nanorods through $Z\mathrm{n}S_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ , thus reducing charge recombination probability and increase Voc in core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs-based ETL (Fig. 4d), which can be conceived by considering the energy band alignment diagram of the strain-free device.
Fig. S22 shows schematic illustration of the energy band diagram of the strain free device with $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods or core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs as ETL. It was demonstrated that a $p$ -n heterojunction structure is formed at ETL/perovskite interface and can be explored by considering the Anderson model to analyze the effect of the interface structure on carrier transport and charge recombination mechanism [67]. According to Anderson model, ETL with wide band gap represents an $n$ -type semi­ conductor whereas narrow bandgap perovskite represents a $p$ -type semiconductor. The two semiconductors have different band gap $(E g)$ , electron affinity $(\chi)$ , dielectric constant (ε) and work function $(\phi)$ . The contact between isolated $n$ and $p$ -type results in an abrupt $p{-}n$ hetero­ junction and the equilibrium energy band diagram is shown in Fig. S22. It is worth noting that in the control device, a $p{-}n$ heterojunction is formed at ZnO/perovskite interface whereas in core-shell ETL the het­ erojunction is formed at ZnS/perovskite interface and type II band structure with staggered alignment is formed at $z\mathrm{ns}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ interface, since the conduction and valence bands of ZnS are higher than the corresponding energy level of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (Fig. $\mathsf{4b}\dot{}$ ) [68–70]. The degree of bending depends on the built-in potential $(V_{D})$ , which is defined as: $V_{D}=$ $V_{D N}+V_{D P},$ where $V_{D N}$ and $V_{D P}$ is the $V_{D}$ of $n$ -type semiconductor and $p$ -type semiconductor which can be determined by resolving the Pois­ son’s equation near interface and thus results in general expression for the built-in potential; $q V_{D}=\phi_{P}-\phi_{N}$ , where $q$ is the electrical charge, $\phi_{p}$ and $\phi_{n}$ are the work function of perovskite and $\mathtt{E T L}$ , respectively. Thus, the bend bending degree is dependent on the difference of work function $(\phi)$ between $p$ and $n$ -type semiconductors [71].
Following Anderson rules of electron affinity $\boldsymbol{(\chi)}$ , the conduction band offset $(\Delta E c)$ , which is caused from the discontinuity of the con­ duction band edges, equals the difference in electron affinity of $n$ and $p$ - type semiconductor and can be expressed as: $\Delta E_{c}=\chi_{N}-\chi_{P}$ , whereas the valence band offset $(\Delta E\nu)$ can be calculated using the equation: $\Delta E_{\nu}$ $=\Delta E_{g N}-\Delta E_{g P}-\Delta E_{c}$ . In Fig. S22, the blue line connecting the con­ duction band edges and valence band edges of perovskite and ETL shows $\Delta E c$ and $\Delta E\nu$ . In present case, $\chi_{p}<\chi_{n}$ and $\phi_{p}>\phi_{n},$ which leads to the formation of a cliff structure at ETL/perovskite interface, which is consistent with earlier report [62]. At $p{-}n$ heterojunction with a cliff structure, the barrier to be overcome by the electrons transported through the interface is $\Delta E_{c}+{\sf q}V_{D},$ which is higher in $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods than core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs. This shows that charge recombination is further suppressed with $z\mathrm{n}s$ interlayer, forming a smooth pathway for the flow of charge, resulting in high performance as shown in Fig. 4d. Furthermore, when an external strain is applied, the energy band alignment is modulated at the ETL/perovskite interface due to the pie­ zophototronic effect as shown in Fig. 7d–i.
Briefly, when an external strain is applied on flexible PSC device under illumination, piezoelectric potential is developed at the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}\mathrm{{/}}}}$ perovskite interface, modulating the energy band alignment at the interface via the piezo-phototronic effect [6]. Figs. 5a and 6a show the effect of the piezoelectric potential on the output performance of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods-based ETL with tensile and compression strain of the device, respectively. The strain-free device yields a Jsc $21.40\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ , Voc $(0.89{\mathrm{V}})$ and PCE of $11.97\%$ . It can be observed that Jsc increases to $24.10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with tensile strain and decreases with compression strain, but Voc is marginally affected, as Voc is decided is decided by quasi fermi level at the junction and is dependent on the illumination intensity, charge carrier concentration, and bandgap, as explained in detail earlier. The improvement in Jsc is directly related to charge car­ rier collection probability and $V_{D}$ at junction, where the higher the $V_{D}$ the higher the charge collection probability and vice-versa. In concave bending (Fig. 7d), the device experienced tensile strain along the axial direction, giving rise to a positive piezoelectric potential at the $Z n O/-$ perovskite interface (Fig. 7e) [62]. The positive piezopotential lowers the conduction and valence bands in the junction region, building up the $V_{D},$ which results in an increase of Jsc. On the contrary, when the ZnO nanorods device is bent into convex configuration (Fig. 7g), the device experiences compression strain, which induces a negative piezoelectric potential at the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods/perovskite interface (Fig. 7h). The negative piezoelectric potential reduces the probability of charge collection as both the conduction and valence band in the junction re­ gion move upwards, weakening the $V_{D},$ which leads to decrease in Jsc.
The mechanism of the piezo-phototronic effect and the modulation of energy band diagrams are different in core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs/ perovskite compared to $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods/perovskite as ETL in flexible PSC. Figs. 7c and S22b show the energy band diagram of strain free PSC with core-shell $\mathbf{ZnO@ZnS}$ NRs/perovskite. In core-shell ETL, the conduction band of ZnS is closely aligned with perovskite due to relatively lower ΔEc compared to $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods, thus facilitating the extraction and transfer of photogenerated electron and reducing charge recombination probability (Fig. S22b) [36]. The extracted electron is then passed to the respective electrode through the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ core. However, ΔEc at the $z\mathrm{ns}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ interface affects the flow of photogenerated electron toward the respective electrode, as a result Jsc and PCE slightly increase to $21.82\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and $12.94\%$ , respectively, in core-shell ETL (Fig. 4d). Which further increases with tensile strain of $1.5\%$ reaching Jsc $\cdot24.40\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ) and PCE (14.68) (Fig. 5b) and decrease with compression strain (Fig. 6b), due to the piezo-phototronic effect.
When the device with core-shell ETL is bent into concave configu­ ration, the device undergoes tensile strain along the nanorods axial di­ rection, creating a positive piezoelectric potential at the $z\mathrm{ns}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ interface, which upward shift the conduction and valence bands of ZnO nanorods (Fig. 7f) [68,70]. Since ZnS shell is polycrystalline, the piezoelectric effect cannot be measured, however, due to the preferen­ tial orientation of partially aligned grains in the crystal lattice of ZnS, piezoelectric polarization charges are generated in the $z\mathrm{n}s$ shell layer [68]. Even though separation of the photoinduced carriers is marginally enhanced at the ZnS/perovskite interface due to the piezo-polarization charge, the transport of the photogenerated electrons can be effectively enhanced at the $\mathbf{ZnO}/\mathbf{ZnS}$ interface due to the piezoelectric potential in ZnO nanorods. With tensile strain, the positive potential at the $z\mathrm{ns}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ interface raises the conduction and valence bands at the junction, which increases the $V_{D}$ and thus the photoinduced current $\mathrm{(I_{L})}$ in the perovskite is further increased. The effective current (I) registered at the load resistor of the solar cell is calculated using $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}=\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{L}}-\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{:}}}$ Where $\mathrm{I_{F}}$ is the positive current of the $p{-}n$ heterojunction produced by the positive photovoltage. Under short circuit conditions, the positive cur­ rent $(\mathrm{I_{F}})$ is zero, and as a result the effective current (I) is the short circuit current $\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ or $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{J_{sc}}})$ or in other words $\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{sc}}=\mathbf{I}_{\mathrm{L}}$ . Thus, the $J_{s c}$ increases when the device is under tensile strain [72,73].
Conversely, when the device is bent downward into convex config uration (Fig. $\mathbf{7g)}$ , the device undergoes compression strain, creating a negative piezoelectric potential at the $z\mathrm{ns}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ interface. The negative piezoelectric potential re-aligns the energy band level with a downward shift of the conduction and valence bands in the junction region, increasing the barrier height, which weakens the $V_{D}$ at the $z\mathrm{ns}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ interface (Fig. 7i) and thus deteriorating the device performance with compression strain, as confirmed by EIS.
## 2.6. Stability of flexible PSC
To examine the effect of the ZnS interlayer on the thermal stability, analogous devices on glass/FTO substrates were fabricated and the thermal stability was investigated in ambient condition at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ Fig. 8a and b shows digital photographs of the perovskite deposited on $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs at different annealing time. The perovskite film deposited on $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ nanorods was easily decomposed and turned yellow color after annealing for $40\mathrm{min}$ [30]. In, contrast, the perovskite deposited on core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\textcircled{a}}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs remains intact even after $3\mathrm{h}$ annealing in air. The XRD pattern in Fig. 8c shows the change of crys­ talline phases of perovskite on $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods at different annealing times. In the XRD pattern, the peak at $12.6^{\circ}$ indicates $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ formation, which appeared after $20\mathrm{min}$ annealing of the perovskite film on $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods [30,74]. In addition, an extra peak at $11.4^{\circ}$ appeared after annealing for $180~\mathrm{{min}}$ , which corresponds to the ${\tt8–F A P b I_{3}}$ phase, showing a degradation of perovskite. Besides, a similar observation of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ evolution with annealing time is observed by UV–vis spectra (Fig. S18a). In contrast, the XRD (Fig. 8d) and UV–vis spectra (Fig. S18b) show the evolution of crystalline phases of perovskite on core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs at different annealing time. A weak $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peak appears after $3\mathrm{h}$ of continuous annealing in air, indicating excellent thermal stability of perovskite on core-shell $\mathbf{ZnO}@\mathbf{ZnS}$ NRs. The enhanced sta­ bility of perovskite on core-shell $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{ZnO@2nS}}$ NRs is ascribed to the strong coordination of sulfide with $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ forming $\mathbf{\Delta}_{\mathbf{Zn-S-Pb}}$ electronic pathway and thereby suppressing the $-\mathrm{OH}$ group on the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface, resulting in highly stable PSC [75]. The Pb–S interaction is confirmed using an in­ direct approach, where the $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ nanorods substrate with and without $z\mathrm{ns}$ are dipped in $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ solution. While the substrate with only $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods does not show any physical change, whereas the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods with $z\mathrm{n}s$ show the appearance of brown color, indicating the formation of $\mathrm{Pb}{-}S$ bond (Fig. S19). The Pb–S coordination is confirmed by XPS analysis, where Zn, O, S along with Pb peaks are observed (Fig. S20). The peaks at $161.4\mathrm{eV}$ and $160.3\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to the binding energy of S $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ and $\l_{\l}s2\mathrm{p}_{\l_{3/2}}$ , respectively. The $\mathbf{Pb^{2+}}$ peaks at binding energy of 136.6 CE $141.5\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Pb}}~4\ensuremath{\mathrm{f}_{7/2}}$ and Pb $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ , respectively [36,76,77]. The Pb $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ peak ascribes to the formation of Pb–S, indi­ cating that the $z\mathrm{n}s$ interlayer strongly can bind with $\mathbf{Pb^{2+}}$ ions of perovskite layer. Consequently, the as-formed $Z{\mathrm{n}}{\cdot}S{\cdot}P\mathbf{b}$ interface pas­ sivates the surface of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ by reducing surface hydroxyl group as observed in O 1s spectra, which show two peaks at 529.7 and $528.2\:\mathrm{eV}$ assigned to surface hydroxyl group and saturated oxide in core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs. The ratio of hydroxyl group peak to saturated oxide peak is reduced in core-shell $\mathbf{ZnO}@\mathbf{ZnS}$ NRs leading to improved sta­ bility of PSC.
Furthermore, the PCE variation with the thermal and light soaking test is shown in Fig. 8e and f. Fig. 8e shows that after 50 days of storage at ambient conditions of $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with $25\%$ humidity (dry box storage), the device based on core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs retains $90\%$ of its initial PCE, whereas the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorod based devices retained only $66\%$ of its original performance. Fig. 8f reveals that initial efficiency of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ based PSC retains only $21.1\%$ (16 days) by increasing the temperature to $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $(40-60\%$ humidity) whereas the core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO@2nS}$ NRs-based PSC retains $84.2\%$ (30 days) of its initial efficiency under the same condition. Similarly, the light soaking stability of PSC devices under 1.5G illumi­ nation has been evaluated in ambient environment, as shown in Fig. 8g. The PCE of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorod based PSC decreased rapidly to $74.3\%$ of the initial value after light soaking for $300\mathrm{min}$ , whereas the core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO@2nS}$ NRs-based PSC decreased by $\sim11\%$ only. The enhance­ ment of long-term thermal and light soaking stability of the core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO@2nS}$ NRs-based PSC is ascribed to the $\scriptstyle2\mathrm{n-S-Pb}$ pathway and sup­ pression of ‒OH defects at ZnO/perovskite interface, as discussed earlier. The results demonstrate long term stability of core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO@2nS}$ NRs-based PSC compared to recently report flexible PSC as illustrated in Table S5.
## 3. Conclusions
In this contribution, an effective strategy to utilize external me­ chanical strain through the piezo-phototronic effect with ZnS interlayer at the ZnO/perovskite interface is presented. The $z\mathrm{ns}$ interlayer plays dual role by upshifting the conduction band maximum (CBM) and forming strong $Z{\mathrm{n-}}S\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Pb}$ coordination bond at ZnO/perovskite interface for efficient electron transport and reduced ‒OH groups on the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ surface resulting in highly stable PSC. The external strain induced piezoelectric potential in ZnO core, generating piezo-polarization charges in the $z\mathrm{n}s$ shell, which modulates the energy level of the ZnS/ perovskite and $z\mathrm{ns}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ interfaces and thus significantly improves the PCE of the flexible PSC. Furthermore, the mechanism of the piezophototronic effect under various strain forms and its effect on energy level modulation is proposed. Building such a core-shell structure offers a promising path toward stable and high-performance flexible PSC with prospective application in wearable and portable electronic devices.
## 4. Experimental
## 4.1. Materials
Zinc acetate dihydrate (Zn $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ $99.999\%$ ), Ethanol­ amine $(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathrm{NO},~\geq~98.0\%)$ , Hexamethylenetetramine $\mathrm{(C_{6}H_{12}N_{4}}$ $99.0\%$ ), Lead(II) iodide $\mathrm{(PbI_{2},}$ $99.999\%)$ , Lead(II) bromide $\mathrm{(PbBr_{2}}.$ $99.999\%\right)$ , Dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.9\%$ , Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, $99.9\%$ ) and Toluene $(\geq99.5\%)$ were purchased from SigmaAldrich. Zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn~}(\mathrm{NO_{3}})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $98.0\%$ ) and Thio­ acetamide $\mathrm{\C_{2}H_{5}N S}$ , $99.0\%$ ) were purchased from Alfa Aesar and Macklin, respectively. Methylammonium bromide (MABr, $,\ge99.5\%\AA^{\cdot}$ and Cesium iodide $(\mathrm{CsI},\geq99.9\%)$ were purchased from Xi’an p-OLED Corp. Formamidinium iodide (FAI, $99.99\%$ ) was purchased from Greatcell Solar Materials. All materials were used without further purification.
## 4.2. Fabrication of ETL on flexible substrate
Indium tin oxide (ITO)-coated polyethylene terephthalate (PET) with a dimension of $2\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}\times2$ cm was used as flexible substrate. The ITO/PET substrate was sequentially washed with deionized water, acetone, iso­ propyl alcohol, and ethanol ultrasonically and then dried with nitrogen gas. The key to fabricate efficient perovskite solar cells is to ensure the flatness of the substrate during the fabrication process. For this reason, the ITO/PET substrate was attached to glass using polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) as support. PDMS layer is obtained by mixing elastomer (Syl­ gard 184, Dow Corning) and curing agent in 10:1, casting in a petri dish, and annealing at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . After device fabrication, ITO/PET were detached from PDMS for characterization.
ZnO nanorods were grown using a hydrothermal method at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ . The growth solution was an equimolar mixture of zinc nitrate hexahydrate and hexamethylenetetramine (1:1) solution. A compact ZnO seed layer was first deposited on the ITO/PET substrate from the seed solution. The $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ seed solution was prepared by the sol-gel method using zinc acetate dihydrate $(0.35\mathrm{M})$ and ethanolamine (MEA, $0.45~\mathrm{mL}$ ) in ethanol solvent. MEA added in seed solution work as a dispersant and stabilizer. The seed solution was then agitated for $3\mathrm{h}$ at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and aged overnight at ambient conditions. To achieve high seed density and well-aligned $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods on ITO/PET substrate, plasma treatment was applied for $5\mathrm{min}$ to enhance the hydrophilicity. The asprepared $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ seed precursor was spin-coated on the treated substrate in two steps programs: $1000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for 6 s and $3000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $60~\mathsf{s}.$ The seed film was then annealed at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ before hydrothermal growth. The $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods were post-treated hydrothermally with thi­ oacetamide (0.2 M) solution at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 0.5, 1.0, and $1.5\mathrm{h}$ to obtain core-shell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs at different sulfidation time (represented by core-shell $\mathrm{ZnO@ZnS~NRs.X};$ , where $\mathbf{X}=$ sulfidation time). The fabrica­ tion process is schematically illustrated in Fig. 1a.
## 4.3. Device fabrication
For device fabrication of PSC, the prepared $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods and coreshell $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}{\ @}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}$ NRs ETL on ITO/PET substrate were further used. The perovskite film was prepared via a single-step process from a precursor solution containing $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ( $\langle484\mathrm{mg}\rangle$ , $\mathrm{PbBr}_{2}(73.4\:\mathrm{mg})$ , MAI $(22.4~\mathrm{mg})$ , FAI $(172\mathrm{mg})$ in a mixture of $1~\mathrm{mL}$ anhydrous DMF: DMSO (4:1). Next, $42\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of CsI $.1.5\mathrm{M}$ in DMSO) was added to precursor to obtain ternary cation perovskite with a stoichiometric ratio of $\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}(F A_{0.83}M A_{0.17})_{0.95}P b}$ $\left(\mathrm{I}_{2.6}\mathrm{Br}_{0.4}\right)$ represented by Cs/FA/MA. The prepared perovskite was dropped on the ETL modified substrate and spin-coated at $4000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $40~\mathsf{s}.$ . During this procedure, toluene $(150~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was dropped on the spinning substrate $10\:s$ before the program ended and the resulting film was then annealed at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ . Subsequently, the SpiroOMeTAD layer was deposited as a HTL on the perovskite film. Finally, silver (Ag) electrode was fabricated by thermal evaporation as back contact to complete device fabrication.
